  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n \n- Warm conditions continue through Friday. \n\n- Gusty west winds Friday afternoon creating near-critical fire \n  weather conditions across Southwest Montana.\n\n- Sharp cold front Saturday brings a dramatic winter transition. \n\n- Gusty east-northeast winds Saturday night.\n\n- Hard freeze Monday and Tuesday mornings.\n\nHigh pressure continues to keep conditions warm and dry across the\nNorthern Rockies through late week, with temperatures remaining 15\ndegrees above normal through Friday. On Friday afternoon, gusty\nwest-to-southwesterly winds will increase ahead of an incoming\ntrough, generating near-critical fire weather concerns across \nSouthwest Montana.\n\nConfidence remains very high for a complete pattern reversal\nstarting Saturday as a strong cold front plunges southward across\nthe region. As high pressure builds east of the Divide, an east-\nto-northeast pressure gradient will tighten rapidly Saturday night\ninto Sunday morning. This will produce gusty east to northeast\nwinds across pass levels and east-west valleys. Furthermore, model\nsoundings indicate favorable cross-barrier flow and stability\nprofiles for a potential Bora (downsloping wind) event along the \nwestern slopes of the Mission Mountains Saturday night.\n\nPrecipitation and Snowfall Impacts:\nLatest model guidance continues to lock in on the Continental\nDivide and  Southwest Montana for the bulk of the moisture. For \nthe 36-hour window ending Sunday evening:\n\n\tContinental Divide Terrain: 5 to 12 inches of snow expected in \n\tthe mountains along the Divide. \n\n\tMountain Passes: Marias, MacDonald and Homestake Passes are \n\tcurrently projected to receive 2 to 6 inches, which will create \n\thazardous travel conditions.\n\n\tValleys: Valleys situated east of Highway 93 can expect 1 to 3\n\tinches of slushy accumulation, while western valleys closer to \n\tIdaho see lower probabilities.\n\nTemperatures and Hard Freeze:\nThe incoming airmass will bring an abrupt reality check. High\ntemperatures on Sunday will run 15 to 20 degrees below seasonal\naverages\u2014a remarkable 30 to 35-degree drop from Friday's highs.\nCalm winds and clearing skies Sunday and Monday nights will\nsupport strong radiational cooling, leading to a widespread hard\nfreeze across all western Montana valleys on both Monday and\nTuesday mornings. Protective measures for agricultural interests,\noutdoor plumbing, and irrigation systems should be completed ahead\nof this weekend.",
